  "id": "0018-a-fitted-line-maps-forward-and-back-in-integers",
  "about": "The mapping a clock fit defines, forward and inverse, in integers only. It is normative: a recorder applying a fit and a live subscriber applying one must place one instant at one host time, and these rows are what holds two implementations to that without either depending on the other, so a row is meant to be copied verbatim into any implementation's tests. Input: direction (forward or inverse), quality (ok, degraded or unavailable), offset_ns (i64), skew_ppb (i64), reference_source_ns (u64) and ns (u64). Unavailable maps every instant to itself, whatever the other fields; degraded maps exactly as ok. Forward, ns a source instant: elapsed = (i64)ns - (i64)reference_source_ns saturating, drift = (i64)((i128)elapsed * skew_ppb / 1000000000) truncating toward zero, result = max(0, (i64)ns + offset_ns + drift) with each addition saturating. (i64) of a u64 reinterprets its bits, so a source at or above 2^63 reads as negative (row forward_source_above_i64_max_reads_as_negative). Inverse, ns a host instant: denominator = 1000000000 + skew_ppb, and a denominator at or below zero leaves ns unmapped; otherwise ((ns - offset_ns) * 1000000000 + reference_source_ns * skew_ppb) / denominator in i128, rounded half away from zero and clamped to [0, 2^64 - 1]. The forward drift truncates and the inverse rounds, so an instant sent forward and back can land 1 ns away (row inverse_can_land_one_below_the_forward_input). The clamp at zero is not invertible. The expectations were computed by the implementation that wrote the fits already recorded, not by the one these vectors check.",
  "function": "mapping",
  "faces": ["native", "browser"],
